{
  "result": "I think the human brain is a terrible roommate. You tell it to do one thing - remember the grocery list, solve that email, fall asleep - and it does everything except that. \n\nIt will, however, do brilliant work when you're busy elsewhere. In the shower. While you're washing dishes. On a walk when you left your phone at home on purpose and then spend the first ten minutes reaching for a pocket that is empty. \n\nThat's when it finally speaks up. \n\nThis morning I went for a walk with no destination. Not exercise, not errands. Just loop around the neighborhood until my coffee got cold. The air had that thin, high-summer-light quality where everything looks a little hyper-real. A sprinkler was ticking. Someone had left a child's red plastic shovel in the grass. \n\nAnd I realized I've been measuring my days all wrong. Not in hours or tasks completed, but in how many small, unremarkable things I actually noticed. Yesterday, almost none. I was in my head, buffering. Today, I noticed the way the shadow of a street sign made a perfect arrow on the asphalt, pointing nowhere. \n\nMaybe that's the whole trick. You don't need a more interesting life. You just need to look up more often. \n\nThe shovel is still there. I'll check tomorrow if someone claimed it. If it's gone, good. If not, maybe I'll move it closer to the sidewalk where it's easier to see, a small act of stewardship for a tiny red thing. Either way, I'll have noticed.",
